#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068449} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068451} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070003 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x - y}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of both $x$ and $y,$ so we can't solve the equation by integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ at least not straight away. So, how do we find a general solution to the equation?\n\nFirst of all, note that it is possible to separate the variables $x$ and $y$ on each side of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &={e}^{x-y}=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{y}}\\,⟹\\,{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the rule for integration by substitution, we can simplify the integrand on the left-hand side and carry out the integration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={e}^{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ne^y  \t= e^x + C.\n$$\n\nThis is an **implicit form** of the solution since it is not written explicitly as $y=y(x).$\n\nWe can write our general solution in **explicit form** (i.e., in the form $y=y(x)$) by noting that since $e^y > 0$ for all $y$, we may assume that the right-hand side is positive and apply the natural logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dfb65111-b319-54c6-84cc-f60506595a69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6766 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070004 :tutorial/content "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{g(y)} & =\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals and find the solution. Note that for the solution to be valid, we require $g(y)\\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "56fc1c96-3599-5022-8d83-60d4188ccf07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6767 :tutorial/title "First-Order Separable Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206}]}
